          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Goneril directly presents her love for Lear as a true emotional state and then intensifies that avowal through comparisons. This is the expression's clear dominant force, regardless of whether the claim is sincere.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The focal expression states present love but does not itself promise future conduct, renew an obligation, or undertake a relational commitment. Lear's subsequent grant rewards her answer; it does not turn her avowal into an undertaking.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"We make thee lady: to thine and Albany’s issue\\nBe this perpetual.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.95,\"reason\":\"This is a deliberate, extended answer to Lear's formal request, not a reflex-like or affectively discharged verbal response. Its heightened language does not by itself establish expressive-discharge force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Our eldest born, speak first.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love, the expression serves a persuasive and competitive self-presentation: Goneril answers Lear's love test in order to establish that she merits his largest bounty. This consequential claim-making function is not fully captured by T, P, or E.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Beyond all manner of so much I love you.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a formal court setting, Lear asks his daughters to say which loves him most while allocating their dowers. Goneril, instructed to speak first, addresses the focal avowal to her father and expands it into an extravagant comparative declaration.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"unknown\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Goneril undertakes no specific action or continuing obligation through the focal expression. Lear accepts the answer for purposes of the love test and grants her territory, but that practical response is uptake of her asserted merit, not uptake of a promise. The supplied evidence does not settle her sincerity.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"Recognised as William Shakespeare's The Tragedy of King Lear; the supplied title and scene text explicitly identify it.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage establishes the utterance's immediate setting and Lear's response but does not independently settle whether Goneril actually feels the love she avows. Cordelia's contrasting asides do not by themselves prove Goneril's affection absent.\"}"
        }
